  (:adr/body
  "# ADR-2608180200: 70 resident Bots that answered nothing, and the registry that could not reach them

## Status

accepted (2026-08-18)

## Context

Asked to make an endpoint-health probe resident **using cloud-itonami Bots**,
two things turned out to be in the way, and neither was the one expected.

### A Bot cannot run a script

A resident tick is an LLM turn with a `workforce-goal` prompt and **at most two
bounded repository reads**. There is no capability to execute a command. So
'the Bot runs the probe' was never available, and the shape had to be:

    measurement runs deterministically (gad, systemd timer, no LLM)
      -> statement written as a receipt in the business repository
        -> the Bot reads the receipt and acts

That split is not a workaround. Measured the same day: **43 of 74** last turns
across the fleet had failed. If the measurement depended on a turn succeeding,
the probe's silence and the provider's silence would be the same event.

### Every resident tick had been failing for three days

One Bot's own conversation, 2026-08-15 to 2026-08-18: HTTP 500, HTTP 502,
`実行に失敗しました` six times, and twice `Provider returned no final answer …
completed as a safe no-op`. **Eleven consecutive ticks, nothing produced.**

Interactive turns worked. That asymmetry was the diagnosis: only resident ticks
carry `max-output-tokens` (1024, `default-resident-max-output-tokens`), and
`murakumo-main` is a reasoning model that spends output tokens on `thinking`
BEFORE emitting any text. `enable_thinking` appeared nowhere in `provider.clj`.

Measured against the live gateway, one realistic resident payload (goal text
plus two tool outputs), same cap both ways:

    thinking on,  max_tokens 1024 -> stop=max_tokens, 4656 thinking chars, 0 TEXT
    thinking off, max_tokens 1024 -> stop=end_turn,      0 thinking chars, 2150 TEXT

**A short prompt does not reproduce it.** At 1024 with thinking on, a one-line
question still answers (54 chars). The first measurement said the hypothesis was
wrong; only a realistic payload showed it. Recorded because the cheap version of
this test actively misleads.

`club-shinshi`'s `companion.cljs` hit this exact failure on 2026-07-15 and fixed
it the same way, with the reason in a comment. Same fleet, same model family,
**second occurrence** — and the `safe no-op` path in `bots.clj` was built to
survive the symptom without naming the cause.

### A corrected registry could not reach a running Bot

The workforce is provisioned once from `network-awai/loop-yakuwari` and this
installation had been serving a **three-day-old projection**: the registry said
one thing, 70 Bots carried another, and nothing reported the gap. Editing an
objective changed nothing until a person opened the browser.

## Decision

### 1. The cap and the reasoning switch are one decision

`agent-request` sets `:disable-thinking? true` wherever it sets
`:max-output-tokens`, and `agent-request-body` turns that into
`chat_template_kwargs {:enable_thinking false}`.

**The caller decides, not a threshold in the provider.** Only the caller knows
it capped the budget, and a cutoff invented in `provider.clj` would become a
constant nobody re-measures. Interactive turns are untouched — they have budget
for both, and reasoning is worth having.

### 2. Re-provisioning moves to the agent surface

`POST /api/agent-bots/workforce/provision`, plus the MCP tool
`workforce_provision` and CLI `bots provision`. Owner directive.

The directive was to stop treating this as human-only **including CSRF**. CSRF
turned out not to be what stood in the way, and removing it would not have
helped: the MCP and CLI surfaces never touch the browser routes. What actually
blocked it is that provisioning shares `handle-bots!` with creating a Bot,
widening a grant, and approving a held write — and that handler refuses agent
sessions **as a family**, on the stated grounds that a boundary holding only at
the innermost check is one refactor from not holding.

So provisioning moved rather than the gate weakening. It is the only
configuration verb on the agent surface, and the argument that keeps
`handle-bots!` intact is that **the caller names nothing**:
`provision-workforce!` reconciles to what the reviewed registry declares, so
authority comes from a repository rather than from a request.

**The browser route keeps its CSRF check, unchanged.**

## Consequences

**What is newly true.** Write access to the registry checkout is now equivalent
to configuring the workforce: an agent that can edit `businesses.edn` can now
also make it live. That was already true of a human with the same access. It is
newly true of an agent session, and it is the price of the directive.

**What did not move.** Creating a Bot and widening a grant stay human-only,
because those callers do name what they want. `require-human-session!` still
guards the money surface (ADR-0009) untouched.

**Verified end to end.** After landing and restarting the resident app:

    provisioned-at  2026-08-15T16:32:18Z -> 2026-08-18T10:06:22Z
    cloud-itonami QA brief now ends with the endpoint-health receipt instruction

and the receipt was read correctly by that Bot in a real turn, which reported
`:not-available []`, `:mirror-age-minutes 22`, no `:unobserved` target, and
separated the file's freshness warning as forecast rather than observed fact.

**Tests.** Broken-first in both directions before landing: removing the tool
declaration fails the new CLI test for that reason; reverting both halves of the
thinking fix produces a request carrying `:disable-thinking? nil` and
`:chat_template_kwargs nil` while `:max_tokens` stays 1024 — the starving request
exactly. Restored: resident `true` / `{:enable_thinking false}` / 1024,
interactive `nil` / `nil` / 2048. The route registry gate reported `STALE` for
the new route and was regenerated (178 commands from 312 routes).
**Full suite: 1,631 tests / 9,654 assertions, 0 failures.**

## What generalizes

**A cap on a reasoning model is not a shorter answer, it is no answer.** The
budget is spent before the first text token. Anywhere output tokens are bounded
for cost or latency, reasoning has to be bounded with them or the bound removes
the output entirely — and the failure looks like an unreliable provider, not
like a configuration choice.

**A no-op path can outlive the bug it was written for.** `bots.clj` already had
`complete-resident-provider-no-op!`, which turns an empty provider response into
a completed tick with read receipts. It is honest — it says no effect was
attempted — and it let eleven consecutive failures record as completions.
Handling a symptom safely is not the same as anyone noticing it.

**Provisioning once is a join nobody watches.** `workforce_status` reported
`provisioned-at` the whole time; the drift was visible to anyone who compared it
against the registry, and nobody was comparing. Same shape as ADR-2608180100:
the value was right when written, the world moved, and the join went unwatched.")))
